    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2024/12/saucony-guide-18-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: Moderately cushioned and comfortably firm; PWRRUN provides solid impact protection with only mild softness from the PWRRUN+ sockliner, and heel/forefoot feel is more smooth-rolling than plush.",
        "Responsiveness: Low to moderate; the midsole is described as not very bouncy or snappy and can feel plodding on hills, though the shoe is light enough for short uptempo efforts.",
        "Stability: Strong but non-intrusive stability via Center Path geometry, lateral flare/crash pad, and a generally stiff platform; heel security is high with a stiff/partially rigid heel counter and good lockdown.",
        "Durability: Overall expected to be average; some early heel outsole wear was noted but did not rapidly worsen, traction is average with occasional wet-road slipping, and exposed midsole favors road/track use for longevity."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/saucony-guide-18-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The ride is soft and well-cushioned with a smooth rocker, with PWRRUN midsole plus PWRRUN PB sockliner giving comfortable impact protection and a non-firm heel/forefoot feel for easy and long runs.",
        "Responsiveness: It feels slightly to moderately responsive (more smooth than snappy), with the rocker and 6 mm drop helping forward roll, but energy return decreases as the foam starts to die around 200 miles.",
        "Stability: Stability is subtle/light, mainly from the asymmetrical build, wide platform, and heel bevel; raised sidewalls were not very noticeable in use, and support is likely insufficient for runners needing strong pronation control.",
        "Durability: Outsole durability appears somewhat better than the prior version but zonal rubber shows wet/cold traction limits, and midsole longevity is the bigger issue, with expected life closer to ~250 miles than 300+ miles."
      ]
    }
